        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Hattin</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">4 July 1187, after a march that began on 3 July</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> and the village of Hittin, beside the double hill known as the Horns of Hattin, in present-day Israel</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">King Guy</a> of Lusignan, with about 18,000 to 20,000 men including 1,200 knights from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> and 50 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>; the forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Sultanate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, around 40,000 men including about 12,000 regular cavalry</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">Most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> forces were captured or killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> then took fifty-two towns and fortifications, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> surrendered to him on 2 October 1187.</span></div>
