    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1186</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a> becomes king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in right of his wife Sibylla, over the opposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond III of Tripoli</a>'s faction</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1187</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a> raids a Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> caravan during the truce, and in May the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a> are heavily defeated at Cresson</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1187</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> crosses the Jordan on 30 June and besieges <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> on 2 July, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond</a>'s wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eschiva-of-bures">Eschiva of Bures</a> holds the citadel</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1187</span><span class="rf-text">On 3 July <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy</a> leaves La Saphorie and marches east, and the army camps without water on the plateau near Meskenah</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1187</span><span class="rf-text">On 4 July the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> army is surrounded and broken, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy</a>'s tent falls</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1187</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald</a> killed and orders the execution of the captured Templar and Hospitaller knights</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1187</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> surrenders on 5 July, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> surrenders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> on 2 October</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1189</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> begins, two years after the battle</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-hattin" data-v="f9826b58f2" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Crusaders camp on the dry plateau</b> The Frankish army could not reach Tiberias by nightfall and turned towards the springs of Kafr Hattin. The Muslims placed themselves between the Franks and the water, so the Franks camped on the arid plateau, closely surrounded.</li><li><b>Archers harass the Franks and the line advances</b> Gökböri's mounted archers shot at the Franks, who were blinded by smoke. Guy's brother Amalric formed the battle lines, and the army set out for the springs of Hattin, where Saladin's army blocked the way forward and any retreat.</li><li><b>Raymond charges twice towards the lake</b> Count Raymond charged twice to break through to the water at Lake Tiberias. The second charge took him to the lake and he made his way to Tyre.</li><li><b>The infantry flee to the Horns and Guy pitches tents</b> After Raymond escaped, most of the Christian infantry fled onto the Horns of Hattin and took no further part. Guy tried to pitch the tents again to block the Muslim cavalry, while the knights and mounted serjeants fought on.</li><li><b>Three charges and the fall of Guy's tent</b> The Crusaders, surrounded, made three charges on Saladin's position. The Muslims rallied each time and drove them back to the hill, and then Guy's tent fell.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon.jpg" alt="Raynald of Châtillon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ReynaldofChatillon%26PatriarchofAntioch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a> became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1186, in right of his wife Sibylla, after the death of her son Baldwin V. The kingdom was divided between a court faction around Guy, made up of Sibylla and relative newcomers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a>, Gerard of Ridefort and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a>, and a nobles' faction led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond III of Tripoli</a>, who had been regent for the child-king Baldwin V and had opposed Guy's succession. Open warfare was prevented only when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-iv-of-toron">Humphrey IV of Toron</a> swore allegiance to Guy.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> had become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1169, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1174 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1183, and so controlled the southern and eastern flanks of the crusader states. He united his subjects under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>, convincing them that he would wage holy war to push the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> from Jerusalem, although he often made strategic truces with the Franks when problems elsewhere in the Muslim world required it, as he did in 1185.</p><p>In 1187 Raynald raided a Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> caravan while the truce was in place. Saladin swore to kill him and sent his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a> and the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori">Gökböri</a> to raid the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>. In May Gerard de Ridefort and the Templars engaged Gökböri at the Battle of Cresson and were heavily defeated, losing around 150 knights and 300 foot soldiers, who had made up a great part of the military of Jerusalem.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group (CNG), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In late May <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> assembled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan Heights</a> the largest army he had ever commanded, around 40,000 men including about 12,000 regular cavalry, and he crossed the River Jordan on 30 June. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> community of Sarahmul, led by Jamal ad-Din Hajji, unexpectedly allied with him. The army stood as a centre under Saladin himself and two wings, the left under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori">Gökböri</a> and the right under Saladin's nephew al-Muzaffar Umar (Taki ad-Din).</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> army gathered at La Saphorie, a well-watered position with a small castle. It numbered around 18,000 to 20,000 men, including 1,200 knights from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> and 50 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. The levy was extended, on an occasion of extreme threat, to every able-bodied man in the kingdom. According to some European sources there were also lighter cavalry, perhaps 10,000 foot soldiers, crossbowmen from the Italian merchant fleet and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, among them local Turcopoles, hired with money donated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> carried the relic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> as the army's standard. To field this force the Crusaders had reduced the garrisons of their castles.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond</a>, reconciled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy</a>, argued that the army should stay at La Saphorie, that Saladin could not hold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> because his troops would not stay so long from their families, and that he was willing to give up Tiberias for the safety of the kingdom. The Hospitallers also advised against provoking Saladin. Gerard de Ridefort urged an advance, and Gerard and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald</a> accused Raymond of cowardice.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori.jpg" alt="Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori">Muzaffar al-Din Gökböri</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Begtiginids._Muzaffar_al-Din_K%C3%B6kburi._AH_563-630_(AD_1167-1233)._)Arbil_mint._Dated_AH_587_(AD_1191-2),_obverse._Bareheaded_and_draped_bust_left;_name_and_titles_of_Muzaffar_al-Din_K%C3%B6kburi_in_outer_margin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 2 July <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> led an assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond</a>'s fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> while the main Muslim army stayed at Kafr Sabt. A tower was mined and collapsed and his troops stormed the breach, but Raymond's wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eschiva-of-bures">Eschiva of Bures</a> held out in the citadel. Saladin had meant to draw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy</a> away from La Saphorie, and on 3 July the Frankish army set out, harassed constantly by Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>. The Springs of Turan could not supply it with water. At midday Raymond judged that Tiberias could not be reached by nightfall, and he and Guy turned towards the springs of Kafr Hattin, about 6 miles (9.7 km) away. The Muslims placed themselves between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and the water, and the Franks camped on the arid plateau near Meskenah, so closely surrounded that, in one description, "a cat could not have escaped". During the night the Muslims prayed, sang and beat drums, and set fire to the dry grass.</p><p>On the morning of 4 July the crusaders were blinded by smoke and came under fire from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori">Gökböri</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a>, resupplied overnight with 400 loads of arrows. Raymond launched two charges towards the water of Lake Tiberias, and after the second he reached the lake and made his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. Most of the Christian infantry fled onto the Horns of Hattin and took no further part. Many of Guy's soldiers, overwhelmed by thirst and wounds, were killed where they stood or taken prisoner. The knights and mounted serjeants fought on, but after three charges on Saladin's position they were surrounded and broken up. At the end Guy's tent fell.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Hattin&#8221; cites where it covers Siege of Tiberias, Battle, Crusader battle losses:</p><ul><li>John France, Hattin: Great Battles Series (Oxford University Press, 2015)</li><li>David Nicolle, Hattin 1187. Saladin's greatest victory (Bloomsbury USA, 1993)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan.jpg" alt="Guy of Lusignan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_issued_by_Guy_de_Lusignan_during_when_he_was_Lord_of_Cyprus_(Crop).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Museum of the The Order of St. John and The University of Birmingham, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The prisoners included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy</a>, his brother Amalric II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a>, William V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>, Gerard de Ridefort and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-iv-of-toron">Humphrey IV of Toron</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> offered Guy water, which in Muslim culture was a sign that a prisoner would be spared, though Guy did not know it. Guy passed the cup to Raynald, and Saladin struck it from his hands and charged Raynald with breaking the truce. Baha al-Din and some other reports say Saladin killed Raynald himself with one stroke of the sword; others record that he struck him as a signal to his bodyguards to behead him. Saladin told Guy that "kings do not kill kings". Guy was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and released in 1188, and the other noble captives were eventually ransomed.</p><p>All 200 captured Templar and Hospitaller knights were beheaded on Saladin's orders, except the Grand Master of the Temple. Captured Turcopoles were also executed, because Saladin believed them to be converts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, which the law of the Ayyubid state punished with death. The rest of the captured knights and soldiers were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> was supposedly sent to Damascus fixed upside down on a lance. On 5 July Countess Eschiva surrendered the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> and was allowed to leave for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> with her family and possessions. Only some 200 knights escaped the battle, and fifty-two towns and fortifications fell to Saladin's forces.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Hattin&#8221; cites where it covers Crusader battle losses, Crusader kingdom falls to Saladin:</p><ul><li>John France, Hattin: Great Battles Series (Oxford University Press, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known from Muslim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> writers. Ali ibn al-Athir describes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> as despondent and tormented by thirst and quotes the eyewitness account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s 17-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-afdal">al-Afdal</a>. Baha al-Din gives one version of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald</a>'s death, and Imad ed-Din, Saladin's secretary, describes the execution of the knights. Ernoul's chronicle is the source for the effect of the news on the Pope, and the historians Jonathan Phillips and Norman Housley give their views.</p><p>Several points are uncertain. The records of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> council on the night of 2 July are biased by personal feuds among the Franks, so what <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-iii-of-tripoli">Raymond</a>, Gerard and Raynald said is known only in outline. The Franks rumoured, and Ibn al-Athir echoed, that Raymond had agreed with Saladin to be made king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in return for peace; it is unclear whether that was true. Housley suggests that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy</a> took Gerard's advice partly because poisoned political relations led both men to see Raymond's advice as aimed at their ruin, and partly because Guy had spent Henry II's donations on calling the army. The Crusader numbers come in part from European sources, and the figure for the foot soldiers is one given by "some" of them. Saint Nicasius, a Hospitaller later venerated as a Roman Catholic martyr, is said to have been among those executed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat.jpg" alt="Conrad of Montferrat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IsabelaKOnrad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The defeat removed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>' capability to wage war, and Muslims once again became the pre-eminent military power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>, recapturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and most other Crusader-held cities and castles. Jerusalem, defended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibylla-queen-of-jerusalem">Queen Sibylla</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Patriarch Heraclius and Balian, surrendered after Balian negotiated its surrender, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> held out because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat">Conrad of Montferrat</a> arrived and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s siege was repulsed with heavy losses.</p><p>In the West, according to the chronicler Ernoul, the news brought by Joscius, Archbishop of Tyre, caused Pope Urban III to die of shock. His successor Gregory VIII issued the bull Audita tremendi calling for a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> within days of his election, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> enacted the Saladin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithe</a> to raise funds. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> did not get under way until 1189; it was a very successful military operation through which many Christian holdings were restored. Christian control in the Holy Land nevertheless remained vulnerable for decades, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-la-forbie">Battle of La Forbie</a> in 1244, 57 years after Hattin, which marked the final collapse of Crusader military power in Outremer. The battle is also known as the Battle of the Horns of Hattin, after the shape of the extinct volcano beside the field.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 4 July 1187 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s army surrounded and broke the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> after it had marched from a well-watered camp into dry country. Most of the Crusaders were killed or captured, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> fell on 2 October, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>.</em></p>

